    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nThe functional test `async basic operations handles tasks that complete` can fail intermittently on Windows because it waits only 10 ms for a task that uses a 1 ms libuv timer followed by `vim.schedule`.\n\nThe failure was observed in the Windows functional-test job:\n\n- Failed run: https://github.com/neovim/neovim/actions/runs/33869571930/job/101012409746\n- Previous passing run of the same test: https://github.com/neovim/neovim/actions/runs/33866694565/job/101003136177\n\n### Failure\n\n```\ntest/functional/lua/async_spec.lua @ 291:\nasync basic operations handles tasks that complete\n\ntest/functional/lua/async_spec.lua:291: timeout\n```\n\nRelevant code:\n\n```lua\nlocal task = run(function()\n  await(function(callback)\n    local timer = add_handle('timer', vim.uv.new_timer())\n    timer:start(1, 0, callback)\n    return timer\n  end)\n  await(vim.schedule)\n  return nil, 1\nend)\n\nlocal r1, r2 = task:wait(10)\n```\n\nThe same test passed in the immediately preceding Windows run and passed on Linux and macOS ARM in the failing run, which suggests scheduler timing rather than a functional regression.\n\n### Suggested fix\n\nIncrease the wait threshold enough to tolerate Windows runner scheduling jitter, or use a deterministic synchronization mechanism that does not depend on a 10 ms wall-clock deadline.",
